    "turn_idx": 74,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, this is its guerrilla campaign tactics. I was at a street fair, and this is a place where I used to campaign with my children. I used to bring them so they could go get cotton candy and go on the rides. And it's a family event. It's a place, it's a nice thing for families to go to. And so I've gone, I've campaigned there, you know, for every campaign that I've had where you just go in your shaking hands and talking to voters. And I had, you know, one of the extremists who supports my opponent come up to me. I politely told him, you know, I'm talking to voters right now. If you want to do an interview, you can call like my campaign and I'll do an interview with you or maybe later. And he started chasing me around putting a camera in my face. And I interrupted me from when I was trying to talk to people on the street to hear about what they care about. He was frightening me. He was frightening the people that I was talking to. And then he posted this inflammatory video and his rhetoric on social media and got stirred up people in a frenzy. They put my home address on social media and told people to come to my house. Now I'm grateful. The police outside just they're cruising by to make sure that nobody's appearing here. So, you know, no one should go to anybody's house that is outrageous on any side of the aisle. I would never tell anyone to go to whatever house my opponent actually lives in. Campaigning is one thing, but risking the safety of your opponent by your lies is outrageous conduct, and my opponent should be ashamed of himself.",
